     1	# CLAUDE.md
     2	
     3	This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.
     4	
     5	## What this is
     6	
     7	Rodrigo AI: a personal local voice orchestrator, built incrementally phase by phase (see `docs/`). Each phase must ship something functional and testable before the next begins — do not build ahead of the current phase's approved plan. Currently: Phase 1 (foundation) and Phase 2 (local voice) are both implemented, automated-tested, and confirmed working end-to-end with a real human voice test (see `docs/implementation-phase-2.md`). Phase 3 (orchestrator) has not started.
     8	
     9	Hard constraint carried across all phases so far: **no paid API, no third-party service, no cloud inference** — everything runs loopback-only on the local machine. Phase 2 uses fully local models (MLX LM + MLX Whisper + local TTS) for this reason. This may change starting at Phase 10 of the roadmap (multi-model/multi-provider), but never silently — any move to a paid service requires an explicit plan and user sign-off, not an assumption.
    10	
    11	## Commands
    12	
    13	```bash
    14	npm ci                # deterministic install from package-lock.json
    15	npm run dev            # dev mode: compiles backend, then runs backend (port PORT) + Vite (port CLIENT_PORT) concurrently, proxying /api
    16	npm run typecheck      # tsc --noEmit for both frontend (tsconfig.json) and backend (tsconfig.server.json)
    17	npm run build          # tsc -p tsconfig.server.json && vite build -> dist/server + dist/client
    18	npm start              # runs the built server from dist/server/backend/index.js, serving API + static client on one origin
    19	npm test               # tsc -p tsconfig.server.json && node --test dist/server/backend/__tests__/*.test.js
    20	```
    21	
    22	There is no test filter flag wired up — `npm test` always compiles and runs the full suite in `src/backend/__tests__/`. To run a single test file directly during iteration, build once (`npm run build:server`) then `node --test dist/server/backend/__tests__/<name>.test.js`.
    23	
    24	Config is via env vars or a `.env` file (see `.env.example`): `PORT` (default 3000), `CLIENT_PORT` (default 5173, must differ from `PORT`), `HOST` (must be exactly `127.0.0.1` or `localhost` — anything else throws at startup). All three are validated strictly (integers 1–65535, no decimals/text) in both `src/backend/config.ts` and `scripts/dev.mjs` independently, since the dev script validates before either child process starts.
    25	
    26	## Architecture
    27	
    28	**Loopback-only, defense in depth at every layer.** The Host header is validated independently in three places with the same allow-list logic (127.0.0.1/localhost + the app's own ports): `src/backend/hostValidator.ts` (production server), the Vite `strictHostValidatorPlugin` in `vite.config.ts` (dev server), and implicitly by `scripts/dev.mjs` pre-flight checks. Any new network entry point (e.g. the Phase 2 voice endpoints) must extend this pattern rather than bypass it — same-origin Host/Origin checks plus per-session tokens, no permissive CORS.
    29	
    30	**`scripts/dev.mjs` is a hand-rolled process coordinator, not just a script runner.** It exists specifically to avoid orphaned child processes: it validates ports before spawning anything, resolves `tsc`/`vite` binaries directly via `process.execPath` (no shell, no `npx`) to avoid extra shell-spawned layers, tracks children in a `Set`, and on `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM` sends `SIGTERM` to all children with a 400ms grace period before `SIGKILL`. Any process this script or the backend spawns in the future (e.g. the Phase 2 Python MLX worker) needs to be registered the same way and torn down within that shutdown path — this has already been audited once (see `docs/audits/phase-2-audit-result-v2.log`, Achado v2-6) for a case where a subprocess needed more than the 400ms force-kill window.
    31	
    32	**Extension points are pre-declared, filled in per phase** (`src/shared/extensions/`): `IVoiceProvider` (Phase 2 — now implemented: `SayVoiceProvider` and `KokoroVoiceProvider` in `src/backend/voice/ttsProviders.ts`, selected via `TTS_ENGINE` env var, both registered into `ExtensionRegistry` only once the real runtime is available), `IAgent` (Phase 3, still empty), `ITool` (Phase 4, still empty). `ExtensionRegistry` (singleton) reports honest `ModuleStatus[]` to the frontend — a module is never presented as available before it actually is.
    33	
    34	**Voice runtime (Phase 2) lives in `src/backend/voice/`** and is optional at the process level: `voiceRuntime.ts` only constructs the real `MlxWorkerEngine` (spawns `scripts/worker/worker.py`, a persistent Python/MLX process talking NDJSON over stdio) if `.runtime/.venv` and `.runtime/models-manifest.json` exist (i.e. `npm run setup:voice` has run) — otherwise `/api/voice/*` returns a `503` with a PT-BR explanation and the rest of the app keeps working. `sessionManager.ts` enforces one active session at a time with synchronous preemption (a new session immediately supersedes the old one — never makes the user wait out the 10-minute idle timeout), cooperative turn cancellation (never kills/restarts the worker for a normal barge-in — that's reserved for catastrophic failures), and only commits an assistant sentence to the LLM's conversation history after the client confirms it actually finished playing (`POST /api/voice/session/played`) — a turn that gets barge-in'd mid-sentence never makes the model believe it said something the user didn't hear. Test coverage (`src/backend/__tests__/voice.test.ts`) uses `FakeVoiceEngine`/a fake `IVoiceProvider` and never depends on Python/MLX being installed; set `RODRIGO_VOICE_DISABLE=1` when spawning a real server subprocess in a test to skip constructing the real engine regardless of local `.runtime` state.
    35	
    36	Real, measured latency (M5 16GB, `Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507-4bit` + `whisper-small-mlx`): median ~3.2s, p95 ~3.8s from end-of-speech to first audio (`npm run bench:voice`, target was ≤4s/≤8s — met). Default TTS is `TTS_ENGINE=say` using `SAY_VOICE` (default `Felipe (Enhanced)`, not the compact `Luciana` voice from the original plan) — macOS's "Enhanced"/"Aprimorada" quality voices sound noticeably better than compact ones at the same ~0.5s latency, so there's no reason to use compact. `TTS_ENGINE=kokoro` (Kokoro-82M via the already-installed `~/.venvs/tts-say` venv, ~1.2s) remains available as an alternative but is not the default — the user compared compact `say`, `Luciana (Enhanced)`, `Felipe (Enhanced)`, and Kokoro on 2026-09-14 and picked `Felipe (Enhanced)`.
    37	
    38	**Directory layout:**
    39	```
    40	src/backend/    — Node HTTP server (http.createServer, no framework). server.ts does Host validation → API routing → static file serving with path-traversal guards, in that order.
    41	src/frontend/   — vanilla TS + Vite, no framework. main.ts uses AbortController + timeout for fetch, never trusts a hung response body.
    42	src/shared/     — types.ts (strict API contracts, e.g. HealthResponse is a literal-typed {status:'ok', version:'0.0.1'}) + extensions/ (see above)
    43	scripts/dev.mjs — dev process coordinator, see above
    44	docs/           — phase plans (phase-N-vX.md), implementation reports, and docs/audits/ (adversarial audit logs, sha256 hashes of audited plan versions, test/build logs)
    45	```
    46	
    47	`npm start` and `npm run dev` both end up serving frontend + API from the same origin/port set — production via the static-file-serving branch in `server.ts`, dev via Vite's proxy (`/api` → backend port). Keep that same-origin assumption when adding routes; it's load-bearing for the Host/Origin security model above.
    48	
    49	## Development workflow for this repo
    50	
    51	Non-trivial changes go through an adversarial plan audit before implementation, not after — see the `auditoria-plano-codex` skill. In practice for this repo: write `docs/phase-N-vX.md`, run it past Codex CLI (`codex exec -s read-only`) and/or Antigravity CLI (`agy --mode plan`) for adversarial review, only implement once there are no pending high-severity findings, then verify against reality afterward (build/test/manual check) — approval to implement is not the same as "done." Audit prompts and full logs for past rounds live in `docs/audits/`; check there before re-litigating a decision that was already audited (e.g. the `say` vs. Kokoro TTS choice, or the session-preemption design) — the reasoning and rejected alternatives are recorded, not just the final answer.

